.TH swapon 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
swapon \- give the kernel a partition to swap on
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B swapon
.I "device blocks"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B swapon
tells the kernel to use
.I device
for swap, using the first
.I blocks
blocks of it.
.PP
Swap is how more processes run than fit in memory at once: the swapper
writes a whole process out and reads it back when it is next scheduled.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I device
A block device with no filesystem and no partition table on it.
.TP
.I blocks
At least 256.
.SH SAFETY CHECKS
.B swapon
refuses if the first sector ends in the
.B 55 AA
signature of a partition table, or if the second sector begins with the
FUZIX filesystem magic.  Both are there to stop you swapping over
something you wanted.
.SH NOTES
Swap is normally set up by
.B /etc/rc
at boot; there is rarely a reason to run this by hand.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR free (1),
.BR mount (1),
.BR mkfs (1)
